Id: no-server-provider-key
Pattern: (\bTmdbUtils\b|MediaBrowser\.Providers\.Plugins\.|"[0-9a-f]{32}")
Subject: *.cs *.html *.csproj *.props *.sln *.sh *.ps1 *.yml *.yaml
Except: none
Issue: 77

The server ships an application key for its own metadata provider and it is a
public constant in the server's source, so reaching for it costs nothing and is
the first thing anybody writing the first source adapter will find. Taking it
puts this plugin's traffic under the server project's registration, spends a
rate budget that belongs to that project, and makes a third party answerable for
how this plugin behaves. None of that is repaired by the value being available.

Two spellings, and they are the same decision reached from opposite ends.

Naming the server's provider code is the first. `TmdbUtils` is the type that
carries the constant, and the namespace above it is where every other bundled
provider keeps its own, so both are refused rather than only the one that is
convenient today. This plugin talks to a source through its own adapter and has
no reason to reference the server's provider assembly at all.

Copying the value is the second, and it is written as a shape rather than as the
value. A rule naming the key would have to carry that key in this tree and in
its fixture, which spreads a third party's live credential to a second public
repository for no gain in what is caught. The shape is a thirty-two character
hexadecimal string in quotes, which is what these keys look like, so the rule
refuses the server's key and equally refuses an operator's key pasted into the
source by somebody testing. Where the key comes from is one code path and that
path reads configuration, which is #77's second condition and is not this rule.

The subject reaches the page, the project files and the workflows as well as the
source. A key pasted into a workflow as a literal, or into the configuration
page's markup, leaves no trace in any `.cs` file, and the rule would report a
clean tree while the value was in the package.

Nothing is excepted. The subject names the file types this plugin's code, its
page, its build and its workflow steps are written in; a rule file is none of
them, so the pattern does not reach the place it is written down.

What it cannot do, and the list is longer than for most rules here. It reads
tracked text one line at a time, so a value assembled from pieces, read out of
an environment variable the runner supplies, or decoded at run time is invisible
to it. It judges length and alphabet rather than meaning, so a credential that
is not thirty-two hexadecimal characters - a base64 token, a bearer string, a
longer key - passes it, and a legitimate thirty-two character hexadecimal
constant would be refused for a reason that is not about a key. And it says
nothing about a key that arrives the way this plugin intends it to, through
configuration: whether that one is handled well is #80's subject and this rule
does not stand in for it.
